from IsRow.inertia_above_g; N_v is a local unit, u(i^N_v)=1 for inertia,
and u is continuous. For a locally constant cocycle c write
A_c(g)(x)=u(g)x+c(g). This is a homomorphism into affine permutations.
A_c(i)^N_v is a translation; its l^M-th power is identity because B is
killed by l^M. Hence inertia in the affine action is killed by N_v*l^M,
a local unit. Near identity the affine action is trivial. The native tame
inertia theorem says its image is generated by the image of the tame
generator tau_v. If c(tau_v)=0, every affine inertia image fixes 0, so
c(i)=0 for ALL inertia, even if u(i) is nonidentity.

Let C be the module of actual local restrictions, E_tau evaluation at
tau_v, K=ker(E_tau|C), and phi a Frobenius. For c in K both c(gamma_v)
and c(phi^-1 gamma_v phi) are zero. The two expansions of c(gamma_v phi)
give u(gamma_v)c(phi)=c(phi); thus E_phi maps K into
ker(u(gamma_v)-1 : B^n->B^n). Evaluation at phi is injective on K:
the zero set of a continuous cocycle is a closed subgroup, contains
inertia and phi if E_phi=0, hence is the whole decomposition group.
It follows from the exact length sequence of E_tau that
  length C <= n*b + length ker(u(gamma_v)-1 on B^n)
            <= n*b + addVal_O det(u(gamma_v)-1).
The second step is Smith normal form: each diagonal entry d_j costs
min(v(a),v(d_j)), bounded by v(d_j), with infinite valuation allowed.
For the actual gamma_v the determinant cost is finite by IsRow.
This also explains why merely counting two arbitrary generator values
